   "s": 1765.2,
   "e": 1821.83,
   "sentences": [
    {
     "t": "In 1974, psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman ran an experiment with a rigged wheel of fortune.",
     "s": 1765.2,
     "e": 1773.33
    },
    {
     "t": "They spun it in front of participants; the wheel was fixed to stop at either 10 or 65.",
     "s": 1773.33,
     "e": 1779.5
    },
    {
     "t": "Then they asked an unrelated question: what percentage of African nations are members of the United Nations?",
     "s": 1779.5,
     "e": 1786.68
    },
    {
     "t": "People who watched the wheel stop at 10 gave a median answer of 25%.",
     "s": 1786.68,
     "e": 1791.88
    },
    {
     "t": "People who watched it stop at 65 gave 45%.",
     "s": 1791.88,
     "e": 1795.87
    },
    {
     "t": "A number they knew was random, from a wheel they had just watched being rigged, still pulled their estimates toward it.",
     "s": 1795.87,
     "e": 1803.41
    },
    {
     "t": "They called the effect anchoring: the first number you hear becomes the reference point your mind adjusts from, and the adjustment is almost never enough.",
     "s": 1803.41,
     "e": 1812.83
    },
    {
     "t": "The experiment became a cornerstone of their 1974 paper \"Judgment under Uncertainty\" and, decades later, of Kahneman's Nobel Prize.",
     "s": 1812.83,
     "e": 1821.83
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "s": 1821.83,
   "e": 1884.13,
   "sentences": [
    {
     "t": "You meet anchoring every time a number is named before you have formed your own.",
     "s": 1821.83,
     "e": 1827.17
    },
    {
     "t": "In insurance it is the default sum insured on the screen, the agent who opens with a â‚¹1 crore cover, the renewal premium quoted against last year's figure: each one quietly sets the range of what feels reasonable, whatever your family's actual needs calculation would say.",
     "s": 1827.17,
     "e": 1844.25
    },
    {
     "t": "In markets it is the grey market premium treated as a listing-day prediction, or last week's price treated as this week's value.",
     "s": 1844.25,
     "e": 1852.58
    },
    {
     "t": "The decision rule the research suggests is simple: before you hear their number, write down your own.",
     "s": 1852.58,
     "e": 1859.13
    },
    {
     "t": "Decide what cover you need from your income and liabilities before you look at the quote; decide what an IPO is worth to you before you read the premium.",
     "s": 1859.13,
     "e": 1868.23
    },
    {
     "t": "The anchor loses its grip when your number arrives first.",
     "s": 1868.23,
     "e": 1874.2
    },
    {
     "t": "Sixty-second recall: a random number on a rigged wheel moved expert guesses by 20 points; the first figure you hear will move yours too, unless yours already exists.",
     "s": 1874.2,
     "e": 1884.13
    }
   ]
